/**
  @page examples-io-medit Converting a MEDIT mesh
  @brief Write a mesh, load it again and convert it to MFEM format.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section examples-io-medit-intro Mesh data and file formats

  A mesh file stores coordinates, element connectivity and integer attributes.
  MEDIT is used by MMG for mesh exchange. MFEM provides another text representation
  of the same basic mesh data. Both formats use the `.mesh` extension; the
  format argument selects the reader or writer.

  This program creates a unit-square mesh with 25 vertices and 32 triangles,
  saves it in MEDIT format and converts the saved mesh to MFEM format.

  @section examples-io-medit-load Write and reload the mesh

  Five vertices in each coordinate direction give four intervals. Scaling by
  one quarter places the grid on the unit square.

  @snippet IO/MEDIT.cpp medit-write

  The output is `Square.medit.mesh`. The program takes no arguments and writes
  into its working directory.

  @section examples-io-medit-convert Convert the loaded mesh

  Loading fills a new mesh object. Saving that object with a different format
  performs the conversion; changing the filename extension alone leaves the
  stored representation unchanged.

  @snippet IO/MEDIT.cpp medit-convert

  The program reloads `Square.mfem.mesh` and compares its vertex and cell counts
  with the original. It returns `EXIT_FAILURE` if this count check fails.
  Applications exchanging curved geometry or specialized metadata should also
  check that their required features are supported by both formats.

  @section examples-io-medit-mmg Continue with MMG

  The @ref examples-mmg-simple_io "MMG I/O example" covers loading and saving
  meshes through the MMG integration. Conversion through the generic mesh
  interface is useful when only the supported geometry, connectivity and
  attributes are required.

  @section examples-io-medit-full Build and run

  Build the `Rodin_IO_MEDIT` example target and run the resulting executable
  from a scratch directory. The expected output reports 25 vertices,
  32 triangles and a successful mesh count round trip.

  The complete program is available in @ref IO/MEDIT.cpp "MEDIT.cpp".

  @section examples-io-medit-see-also Next steps

  - @ref examples-io-mfem "Save and reload a finite element field"
  - @ref examples-io-xdmf "Export a time series for ParaView"
  - @ref guides-io-meshformats "Supported mesh formats"

  @example IO/MEDIT.cpp
  @m_examplenavigation{examples-io-medit,IO/}
  @m_footernavigation
*/
